# Sector-specific economics; cited news selection remains confined to the own-date feed.
SECTOR=[
('XLK',r'Microsoft|Apple|software|technology|AI spending','Technology earnings depend on monetisation keeping pace with investment. Separate infrastructure demand from the software supplier whose customer can reduce seats or delay deployment. A broad AI budget is not automatically a profitable order for every participant.','The margin test is whether recurring revenue, retention and cash generation improve without an equally large increase in selling or infrastructure costs.','Prefer companies with funded demand and internal cash generation. A cheaper discount rate helps valuation but cannot repair weak product economics.','The contrary signal would be strong spending accompanied by disappointing cash returns; that would favour suppliers with enforceable contracts over businesses dependent on discretionary adoption.'),
('XLC',r'Meta|Alphabet|Google|Netflix|Disney|advertis|streaming','Communication businesses join an advertising cycle to a distribution and content model. Audience engagement matters economically only when it becomes pricing, retention or profitable transactions.','Read the mix of advertising yield, paid subscriptions and content costs rather than treating every revenue beat as equally valuable.','The attractive surprise would be operating leverage from an existing audience without a matching step-up in content or customer-acquisition expense.','Strong engagement with weak monetisation would challenge the bullish narrative; a smaller audience with better cash conversion could prove more valuable than a headline growth story.'),
('XLF',r'bank|JPMorgan|Goldman|Morgan Stanley|credit|Visa|Mastercard','Financials require a view on borrowers and capital markets as well as the yield curve. A steeper curve helps some balance sheets but can coexist with deteriorating credit quality.','Distinguish fee growth and capital returns from gains that depend on a temporary funding advantage. Credit provisions and borrower behaviour determine how much of the revenue reaches shareholders.','Prefer well-capitalised lenders and payment franchises where healthy transactions or distributions support the earnings case.','The contrary development would be loan demand improving while credit losses accelerate; that would make apparent growth a lower-quality asset rather than an unqualified catalyst.'),
('XLE',r'oil|crude|Exxon|Chevron|OPEC|energy','Energy combines commodity exposure with capital discipline. A higher product price benefits producers differently from refiners and consumers, and supply uncertainty can reverse quickly.','The useful result is cash generation after maintenance and growth investment, rather than production growth purchased at the expense of returns.','Prefer low-cost balance sheets that can fund distributions through a less favourable commodity environment. Treat political supply headlines as volatility catalysts with a two-way outcome.','Energy equities failing to confirm stronger crude would suggest investors doubt the durability of margins; weaker crude with resilient cash returns would support a company-specific opportunity.'),
('XLI',r'industrial|Caterpillar|Boeing|Airbus|GE Aerospace|Honeywell|manufactur','Industrials depend on converting demand into delivery. A backlog is valuable when pricing, supply chains and working capital allow orders to become cash.','Separate volume growth from mix, timing and cost recovery. Strong reported sales can hide pressure if inventory and receivables absorb the benefit.','Prefer firms with service revenue, disciplined execution and customers able to finance committed projects.','A slowing order book alongside strong reported shipments would warn that delivery is consuming the backlog faster than new business replaces it; that is a different signal from a durable expansion.'),
('XLY',r'Amazon|Tesla|retail|consumer spending|Home Depot|Walmart','Discretionary spending reflects household confidence, financing costs and the trade-off between goods and experiences. Large retailers can take share without proving that the whole consumer is strong.','Distinguish profitable volume from discount-driven sales. Inventory, returns and fulfilment expenses decide whether stronger demand improves earnings.','The better setup is a business with repeat demand, flexible costs and an ability to capture financing relief without depending on a universal spending boom.','Strong sales with weaker margins would challenge the optimistic consumer thesis; modest sales with better inventory discipline could produce a more durable earnings surprise.'),
('XLP',r'staples|Procter|Coca-Cola|Pepsi|Unilever|Walmart|Costco|food prices','Staples offer repeat demand but are still exposed to volume, price and product mix. Consumers can switch brands or pack sizes when budgets are tight.','A price-led revenue increase deserves a different multiple from growth supported by healthy units. Watch whether efficiency offsets input costs without weakening the franchise.','Prefer suppliers with durable distribution and a credible volume recovery rather than relying entirely on defensive market positioning.','A defensive premium can erode if the earnings protection comes only from past price increases; recovering units with stable margins would be the stronger positive surprise.'),
('XLV',r'healthcare|health care|Eli Lilly|Novo|UnitedHealth|Pfizer|Merck|drug|pharma','Healthcare combines product-specific demand with reimbursement, regulation and development risk. A sector label hides large differences between insurers, drug developers and device companies.','The market needs to distinguish recurring operating performance from a single trial, approval or reimbursement headline. Cost trends are particularly important where revenue adjusts slowly.','Prefer visible demand, funded development and a realistic path from scientific progress to cash generation.','An impressive product headline with weaker commercial economics would challenge the easy growth story; better cost control in a neglected established franchise could offer a less crowded improvement.'),
('XLB',r'materials|copper|steel|aluminium|aluminum|mining|Freeport|gold miner','Materials should be analysed by product, cost and end market. Copper is an investable industrial and infrastructure input; it is not a shortcut for declaring the direction of global growth.','Volume, realised prices and operating costs determine the result. A commodity rally may not reach earnings if energy, labour or project expenses consume it.','Prefer efficient producers with manageable capital commitments and identifiable demand from their actual customers.','Higher product prices with weaker margins would challenge the simple commodity-beta thesis. Better cash discipline through a flat price environment would support a more resilient company-specific case.'),
('XLRE',r'real estate|housing|mortgage|REIT|property|home sales','Real estate needs a distinction between lower financing costs and healthier property cash flow. A cheaper debt market cannot by itself repair vacancy or an unsustainable development budget.','Watch occupancy, rents, refinancing and disposal proceeds together. A valuation gain without cash conversion leaves the equity dependent on market confidence.','Prefer assets with durable tenant demand, measured leverage and a refinancing schedule that does not require a perfect funding window.','Falling yields with weak property shares would warn that investors see an operating problem; stable financing alongside improving occupancy would be a firmer basis for adding risk.'),
('XLU',r'utility|utilities|power demand|electricity|nuclear|Duke|NextEra','Utilities and power suppliers combine duration sensitivity with investment needs. Demand for electricity can create an opportunity while also requiring financing and regulatory consent.','The key earnings question is who pays for new capacity and how quickly the investment earns an acceptable return. Large spending commitments are not automatically distributable cash.','Prefer clear cost recovery, credible funding and demand backed by contracts rather than a broad extrapolation of the AI power narrative.','A rally in long-duration assets with weak utility cash generation would challenge the defensive label. Funded demand and predictable recovery would justify a more constructive stance even without lower yields.'),
('SXAP',r'European auto|Volkswagen|BMW|Mercedes|Renault|Stellantis|automaker','European autos face a combination of pricing, product mix and geographic exposure. Unit growth can be bought through incentives, so shipment strength needs a margin test.','Trade costs, electrification spending and competitive pricing can change the value of reported volumes. Assess management guidance against the ability to finance that transition internally.','Prefer balance-sheet resilience and models with defensible customer demand over a simple bet on a regional production rebound.','A policy relief rally without improving orders would be fragile; a modest volume result with stronger mix and controlled investment could provide the more useful positive surprise.'),
('SOXX',r'Nvidia|AMD|TSMC|ASML|Micron|Intel|semiconductor|chip','Semiconductors require a map of where demand becomes profit: design, fabrication, memory and equipment have different bottlenecks and different capital intensity.','Separate AI demand from mature end markets, and orders from regulatory permission to fulfil them. A customer announcement can help the narrative without resolving supply or export constraints.','Prefer suppliers with defensible economics and a funded customer programme. Treat uncertain export opportunities as optionality rather than guaranteed revenue.','Strong spending with uneven supplier margins would undermine a blanket sector call; improving cash returns beyond the most visible AI winner would make the advance more durable.')]
sectors=[]
for tk,rx,narr,reported,watch,contrary in SECTOR:
    expr=tk if tk!='SXAP' else 'EXV5.DE'
    sectors.append({'key':tk,'stance':stance(expr),'parts':[
      part('Narrative',q(rx,'sectors')+narr+' '+obs(expr,(20,),tk if tk!='SXAP' else 'European auto ETF')),
      part('Reported',q(rx,'earnings','result')+reported),
      part('EPS Upside Watch',q(rx,'earnings')+watch),
      part('⚡ Breaking Convention',q(rx,'sectors')+contrary) ]})

earnings={'title':'Cash conversion sets the hurdle for the next earnings premium','parts':sourceparts([
('Rewarded',r'Nubank.*profit|DBS.*earnings beat|DoorDash shares rise|Sony Earnings.*Profit|Treasury Wine profit rises','earnings','result',"The result worth paying for combines demand, margins and a credible financing plan. Revenue acceleration is most valuable when it improves operating cash rather than requiring an equally large increase in investment or customer support. A share-price reaction is useful evidence of expectations, but its durability depends on what changed in the forward earnings path. Prefer an identifiable improvement in recurring economics to a broad claim that an entire industry has entered a new regime."),
('Punished',r'earnings.*(?:fall|drop|miss|slump)|(?:fall|drop|miss|slump).*earnings|guidance.*(?:cut|disappoint)|profit.*(?:fall|drop)','earnings','result',"The important distinction is between a temporary delivery or accounting issue and a weaker demand or margin structure. A cheap-looking multiple offers little protection when the earnings denominator is falling. Companies that disappoint on cash conversion need a clearer operating repair before a recovery trade deserves core position size. Avoid treating an analyst's longer-term opportunity estimate as evidence that the near-term funding and execution problem has been solved."),
('Pattern',r'earnings|results|profit|guidance','earnings','result',"Read reactions across business models rather than counting headline beats. A bank's capital distribution, a software firm's recurring revenue and a manufacturer's order conversion are different forms of shareholder support. The shared test is whether management can explain how the next unit of revenue becomes cash. A modest result with less uncertainty can be worth more than spectacular growth accompanied by a larger financing burden. That framework also explains why apparently similar earnings headlines can produce opposite price responses."),
('Attention into EPS',r'AI.*(?:revenue|earnings|profit)|(?:revenue|earnings|profit).*AI|Nvidia|Palantir|CoreWeave','earnings',None,"A popular story creates an expectations hurdle before it creates an earnings advantage. Ask which customer pays, when the contract converts and what capital is required to deliver it. Attention helps distribution and financing, but neither should be confused with an automatic increase in per-share value. The opportunity is strongest where the operating result improves faster than the expectations embedded in valuation, rather than where the promotional language becomes more confident."),
('Playbook',r'earnings|guidance|quarterly results','earnings',None,"Own demonstrated cash generation and keep uncertain earnings inflections smaller until results validate them. Add after a clear operating improvement rather than because a stock falls on an ambiguous headline. Trim when strong reported revenue is accompanied by weaker margins, stretched working capital or a more demanding financing plan. The invalidation is concrete: a deterioration in repeat demand or cash conversion changes the earnings case even if the long-term industry narrative remains attractive.")])}
underowned=[]
for title,rx,thesis,instrument,risk in [
('Cash conversion behind the infrastructure buildout',r'data cent|power demand|electricity|AI infrastructure|grid',"Investment headlines direct attention toward visible capacity. The less obvious opportunity is the supplier that turns an existing installed base into repeat service revenue without financing the whole project itself.","Consider selective industrial and power-equipment equities; use diversified sector exposure when the contract economics are unclear.","Require contracted demand and improving cash conversion; a longer backlog without funded execution would weaken the thesis."),
('Domestic earnings beneath the policy debate',r'Poland.*(?:growth|consumer)|PKB|sprzedaż|produkcj|Dino|Allegro',"The policy conversation can obscure differences between businesses already earning a return on domestic demand and those waiting for credit to revive. That creates an analytical gap rather than proof of a measured positioning gap.","Selective Warsaw consumer and financial equities express the local earnings thesis more directly than a broad exporter basket.","Look for profitable volume and stable financing; a currency or fiscal shock would require a smaller allocation."),
('Quality within financial easing',r'credit|bank|loan|corporate bond|high yield',"Falling financing costs can lift weak borrowers and strong lenders together for a time. The more durable opportunity may be balance sheets able to grow without sacrificing credit discipline.","Quality lenders, payment franchises and measured credit exposure provide different ways to express that distinction.","The trigger is improving demand alongside stable asset quality; cheaper money with deteriorating borrower economics would reverse the call."),
('Defensive cash flow without a defensive price',r'healthcare|health care|staples|dividend|pharma|insurer',"Popular growth stories can command attention while established franchises work through a specific cost or product issue. The opportunity depends on a visible repair, not on assuming that neglected means cheap.","Use selected healthcare or staples equities where the cash-generation case survives conservative operating assumptions.","Improving margins and repeat demand would validate the trade; regulatory costs or lost pricing power would invalidate it.")]:
 underowned.append({'title':title,'parts':[part('Attention Gap',q(rx)+thesis),part('Exposure & Risk',q(rx)+instrument),part('Trigger to Watch',q(rx)+risk)]})

crypto={'title':'Token demand, financing and equity wrappers need separate risk budgets','parts':sourceparts([
('Market Context',r'Bitcoin|Ethereum|Ether|crypto','crypto',None,"Crypto prices combine risk appetite, access and the supply of investable instruments. A spot token, an exchange equity and a corporate treasury vehicle are different investments, even when they share the same popular narrative. The token has no corporate financing structure, while the equity wrapper adds expenses, dilution and management decisions. Treat a rally in the wrapper as a claim on both the asset and the capital-market mechanism used to hold it."),
('New / Most Important',r'Ethereum|Ether|Bitcoin|crypto ETF|stablecoin','crypto',None,"The relevant question is whether demand is broad enough to survive a change in funding conditions. Regulatory access and distribution can improve the investment channel without eliminating volatility or guaranteeing an immediate economic use case. Distinguish direct asset demand from buying driven by a temporary equity premium. Treasury companies require a per-share analysis: adding assets can help shareholders or dilute them depending on the financing terms. The same bullish token view can therefore justify owning the underlying asset while avoiding a richly priced corporate wrapper."),
('Playbook',r'Bitcoin|Ethereum|Ether|crypto','crypto',None,"Keep core exposure in instruments whose economics match the thesis and size speculative wrappers separately. A sustained improvement in demand and orderly financing would support participation; a deteriorating premium, repeated dilution or a loss of liquidity would argue for reducing equity wrappers first. Use the token's own price behaviour to test the market view, while using financing and balance-sheet disclosures to test the company view. Avoid borrowing against an asset whose volatility can force liquidation before the longer-term thesis has time to develop.")])}
crypto['parts'][0][1]+=obs('BTC-USD',(1,20),'Bitcoin')+obs('ETH-USD',(1,20),'Ether')+obs('SOL-USD',(1,20),'Solana')
flows={'title':'Financing relief must reach borrowers and corporate cash flow','parts':sourceparts([
('Global Liquidity',r'Federal Reserve|\bFed\b|PBOC|ECB|central bank|liquidity','flows',None,"Liquidity is useful when it makes funding available to borrowers with viable uses for it. A policy signal can lift asset prices before it produces additional lending or investment, and that gap is where fragile rallies develop. Separate the price of money from the willingness to extend balance sheets. An easier front end can support duration while weak credit demand leaves the real economy unchanged. For portfolios, the key confirmation is healthier cash conversion and investment rather than a larger volume of optimistic policy commentary. Retain flexibility until those channels agree, because a funding rally and an earnings recovery require different instruments."),
('Rates & Dollar',r'Treasury|bond yields|dollar|\bFed\b','flows',None,"The dollar and the curve transmit US policy expectations into global financing conditions. A weaker dollar can ease pressure on foreign borrowers but also reflect concerns about domestic policy credibility; the equity implication depends on which mechanism dominates. Long-duration assets benefit most cleanly when real financing pressure falls without a deterioration in cash flows. If the front end rallies while longer-term yields remain demanding, mortgage-sensitive and capital-intensive businesses still face a hurdle. Keep the interest-rate exposure explicit instead of treating every risk asset as a substitute for a duration position."),
('Credit Check',r'corporate bond|high.yield|credit|debt|bank lending','flows',None,"Credit is the bridge between a reassuring macro narrative and the ability to fund operations. Price stability in a bond fund is useful context, but it does not by itself prove that every borrower has access on reasonable terms. Examine whether debt supports productive activity or merely extends the life of a weak capital structure. Refinancing can buy time without improving enterprise value. A broader equity rally deserves more trust when credit quality, issuance and corporate cash generation reinforce it. If they diverge, reduce the weakest balance sheets before cutting franchises that can fund themselves internally."),
('Equity & Fund Flows',r'fund flows|ETF.*inflow|ETF.*outflow|buyback|investor.*(?:buy|sell)|foreign.*(?:flow|buy|sell)','flows',None,"Flows can amplify a move, but the character of the buyer matters. A systematic allocation, a corporate repurchase and a discretionary investor reacting to a headline do not have the same horizon or price sensitivity. Do not extrapolate a single flow observation into a permanent source of demand. The durable signal would be continued investment accompanied by better earnings expectations and broader leadership. A market dependent on a narrow group of highly visible names can remain liquid at the index level while individual positions become harder to exit. Position size should reflect the liquidity of the instrument actually owned."),
('Portfolio Implication',r'Wall Street|US stocks|global stocks|investors|fund flows','flows',None,"Keep a balanced core of liquid equities with defensible cash flows and maintain room to add when financing and operating evidence align. Duration can hedge a demand slowdown, but it is a less reliable hedge against an inflation or fiscal risk shock. Commodity exposure and foreign currency require their own causal case rather than being added as generic diversification. Reduce leverage when unrelated exposures begin to depend on the same policy outcome. The portfolio improves when each holding has a distinct source of return and a clear reason to exit, rather than several labels attached to the same discount-rate bet.")])}
flows['parts'][1][1]+=obs('DX-Y.NYB',(20,),'DXY')+obs('TLT',(20,),'TLT')
flows['parts'][2][1]+=obs('HYG',(20,),'HYG')+obs('LQD',(20,),'LQD')
anticipatory=[]
for title,rx,base,yes,no in [
('Policy communication versus delivered easing',r'Jackson Hole|Powell|Fed.*(?:expect|preview|outlook)|rate.cut',"The policy trade asks whether communication becomes a credible path to easier financing without worsening inflation expectations.","If softer inflation and stable activity reinforce an easing path, profitable duration and selected domestic cyclicals should benefit through different channels.","If policy relief is delayed or long-term financing remains demanding, reduce the weakest duration-sensitive balance sheets first."),
('Trade accommodation versus demand friction',r'tariff|trade talk|trade deal|trade war',"Trade accommodation reduces the range of possible costs before it improves companies' actual order books.","If greater predictability reaches orders and margins, add exporters with flexible production and a clear customer base.","If costs remain uncertain or concessions fail to reach end demand, favour businesses with domestic pricing power and shorter cash cycles."),
('Infrastructure promise versus funded returns',r'AI.*(?:spend|invest|infrastructure)|data cent|power demand|Nvidia',"Infrastructure optimism is valuable only when someone funds the capacity and customers pay enough to generate a return.","If contracts, delivery and cash generation improve together, suppliers with defensible economics deserve a larger risk budget.","If spending rises faster than the evidence of customer returns, keep capital-intensive or refinancing-dependent exposures smaller."),
('Household relief versus a fragile recovery',r'retail sales|consumer spending|housing|mortgage|employment',"Household relief can arrive through softer prices or financing costs, but confidence determines whether it becomes spending.","If employment remains secure and profitable sales improve, broaden selectively into consumer and housing-sensitive earnings.","If households save the benefit or credit quality deteriorates, prefer repeat demand and internally financed businesses.")]:
 anticipatory.append({'title':title,'parts':[part('What Is Priced',q(rx,'calendar')+base),part('Conditional Expression',q(rx)+yes),part('Risk',q(rx)+no)]})

SCENARIO_DEPTH=[
('Policy communication versus delivered easing', 'The most useful cross-check is whether short-term borrowing costs fall while credit remains orderly. That combination would support easing as insurance rather than emergency support. If investors instead demand a higher premium for long-term funding, a dovish message could coexist with tighter conditions for mortgages and capital-intensive businesses. The portfolio should respond to the financing result, not merely the language.'),
('Trade accommodation versus demand friction', 'Map the exposure through customers, production sites and contract terms before taking index risk. A supplier able to redirect output has more flexibility than one tied to a single destination. The stronger confirmation would be management reporting that customers are placing profitable repeat orders, rather than simply bringing forward shipments to avoid another uncertain policy deadline.'),
('Infrastructure promise versus funded returns', 'Follow the funding chain from the customer to the asset owner and the equipment supplier. A supplier paid on delivery can have a different risk profile from an operator that borrows to build capacity and waits for utilisation. The scenario becomes more attractive when signed demand, delivery schedules and return on invested capital reinforce each other.'),
('Household relief versus a fragile recovery', 'The early evidence should come from the relationship between traffic, basket value and gross margins. A household can trade down while aggregate sales remain respectable, creating winners through market share rather than a general spending boom. Prefer companies able to adjust inventory and costs when demand changes, and ask whether the customer response is sustained beyond a promotional period.')]
for scenario,(_,depth) in zip(anticipatory,SCENARIO_DEPTH):
 scenario['parts'][2][1]+=' '+depth

# Catalyst names are selected from the date's actual company coverage.
CANDIDATES=[('NVDA','Nvidia'),('INTC','Intel'),('PLTR','Palantir'),('CRWV','CoreWeave'),('LLY','Eli Lilly'),('WMT','Walmart'),('TGT','Target'),('HD','Home Depot'),('AMD','AMD'),('COIN','Coinbase'),('TSLA','Tesla')]
catalysts=[]
COMPANY_THESIS={
'NVDA':('Export access is a separate earnings variable from customer demand. An order is most valuable when the company is permitted and able to fulfil it on attractive terms.', 'Nvidia needs demand, delivery and permission to line up. Reduce exposure if policy optionality is being valued as assured cash revenue; retain a measured position where the funded customer base supports the earnings path.'),
'INTC':('Strategic relevance can improve financing options without fixing manufacturing economics. Any investment thesis needs a distinction between support for the company and favourable terms for existing shareholders.', 'Intel requires both capital and execution. A support package that dilutes existing owners excessively would weaken the equity case even if it helps the industrial programme; improving customer economics would provide a more durable confirmation.'),
'PLTR':('The software opportunity needs a conversion from customer enthusiasm to repeat commercial revenue. A valuable product can still be a demanding investment if the price assumes flawless expansion.', 'Palantir deserves exposure only at a size consistent with the expectations hurdle. Reduce the position if bookings or cash conversion fail to support the growth narrative, while recognising that a high valuation alone does not determine the next price move.'),
'CRWV':('Infrastructure growth requires financing before capacity produces a return. Revenue strength and shareholder value can diverge when the capital required to deliver the next contract rises quickly.', 'CoreWeave needs a credible path from funded capacity to operating cash generation. Keep the position small if financing or shareholder supply dominates the price; a stronger customer backlog matters most when it improves returns after investment.'),
'HOOD':('A brokerage benefits from customer activity, but transaction enthusiasm is not a permanent annuity. The earnings case depends on monetisation, retention and the durability of client engagement.', 'Robinhood provides an equity expression of platform economics rather than a direct claim on its customers’ favourite assets. Reassess if revenue depends increasingly on episodic speculation or customer acquisition becomes less profitable.'),
'LLY':('Commercial growth needs to survive the relationship between pricing, access and delivery. Strong demand is valuable when capacity and reimbursement allow the company to capture it profitably.', 'Lilly requires attention to realised economics rather than demand headlines alone. A deterioration in pricing or reimbursement without a volume offset would change the thesis; improved availability with disciplined returns would strengthen it.'),
'WMT':('Scale can help a retailer gain customer spending while the wider household remains selective. The crucial question is whether sales strength reaches margins and cash flow.', 'Walmart is a consumer execution thesis, not a universal statement that households are healthy. Add when profitable demand confirms the operating case; reduce if costs or discounting consume the benefit of greater volume.'),
'TGT':('A retailer needs customers to choose its assortment at a profitable price. Leadership and strategy matter only when they reach traffic, inventory discipline and cash generation.', 'Target requires a credible operating repair rather than a familiar valuation reference. A plan that depends on repeated discounts deserves caution; improving inventory and customer demand would offer a clearer reason to increase exposure.'),
'HD':('Housing-linked demand depends on financing, existing homeowners and the willingness to begin projects. Orders need to become profitable sales without leaving excess inventory.', 'Home Depot offers operating leverage to a healthier project cycle, but a lower rate alone is insufficient. Add when customer activity and margins confirm each other; reduce if financing relief fails to reach demand.'),
'AMD':('AI and conventional computing create different earnings opportunities. The investment case depends on delivered competitive performance and customer economics rather than an undifferentiated semiconductor narrative.', 'AMD needs commercial adoption to support the expectations embedded in valuation. Prefer funded deployments and improving margins; reduce exposure when a larger addressable market is accompanied by a less certain return on investment.'),
'COIN':('The exchange business earns through activity and services, while the underlying tokens carry different economics. Regulatory access can improve distribution without making transaction cycles disappear.', 'Coinbase is a platform and operating-leverage position. A token rally with weak platform monetisation would challenge the thesis; sustained activity with controlled costs would support a more durable earnings case.'),
'TSLA':('The equity combines an operating business with expectations for future products. Investors should distinguish revenue that customers already pay from the capital and execution needed for a new opportunity.', 'Tesla requires valuation discipline around long-duration possibilities. Reduce exposure when new commitments increase financing or execution risk without a clearer path to returns; existing operating cash generation remains the anchor for a defensible position.')}
for tk,name in CANDIDATES:
 if len(catalysts)>=6:break
 if not any(re.search(r'\b'+re.escape(name)+r'\b',x['title'],re.I) and not BAN.search(x['title']) for x in items):continue
 rx=r'\b'+re.escape(name)+r'\b'
 thesis,expression=COMPANY_THESIS[tk]
 catalysts.append({'title':'$'+tk+' — '+{'NVDA':'export access must become cash revenue','INTC':'support terms decide shareholder value','PLTR':'commercial growth meets the valuation hurdle','CRWV':'financing must support capacity returns','HOOD':'activity needs durable monetisation','LLY':'demand must survive pricing and access','WMT':'scale meets household selectivity','TGT':'operating repair is the real catalyst','HD':'financing relief needs project demand','AMD':'customer adoption decides the AI premium','COIN':'token enthusiasm meets platform economics','TSLA':'separate operating cash from future options'}[tk],'stance':stance(tk),'parts':[
 part('Catalyst & Thesis',q(rx)+thesis),
 part('Price Hurdle & Risk',obs(tk,(1,20),name)+'The latest price and the operating catalyst need to justify the same expected earnings path for '+name+'.'),
 part('Trade Expression',q(rx)+({'Bullish':'Retain a selective long bias. ', 'Bearish':'For '+name+', avoid adding long exposure until the operating hurdle clears. ', 'Mixed':'Keep the position neutral until the catalyst resolves. '}[stance(tk)])+expression) ]})
assert len(catalysts)==6
